  "description": "The SSL service identified as **ssl_service_1** is bound to TCP port **443** and presents the common name **www.example.com**. It uses the **default_ssl_profile** for its SSL parameters, and both SSL redirect and cipher redirect are enabled; requests that hit a prohibited cipher are sent to **https://example.com/secure**. SSL\u2011v2 redirection is disabled, with the fallback URL **https://example.com/ssl2** provided for completeness, while SSL\u2011v3 remains enabled. TLS\u202f1.0, TLS\u202f1.1 and TLS\u202f1.2 are all permitted, but TLS\u202f1.3 is turned off. The service also advertises support for SSL\u2011v2 (disabled) and SSL\u2011v3 (enabled), with HTTP\u202f2.0 and SNIs both enabled.\n\nOn the cryptographic side, Diffie\u2011Hellman key exchange is enabled, using the parameters file **/etc/ssl/dhparams.pem** and allowing up to **5** DH key exchanges before a refresh. The DH key\u2011exchange size limit is disabled. RSA\u2011based server authentication (ERSA) is enabled with a reuse count of **2**, and non\u2011FIPS ciphers are permitted. OCSP stapling is active, and the strict signature\u2011digest check is also enabled. Client authentication is disabled, although the system will accept optional client certificates if presented.\n\nAdditional operational settings include a push\u2011encryption trigger set to **Always**, DTLS\u202f1.0 enabled (DTLS\u202f1.2 disabled) with the DTLS profile named **dtls_profile**, and the DTLS flag turned on. Session reuse is enabled with a timeout of **300** seconds, and the service will send a close\u2011notify alert (\u201cYES\u201d). Port\u2011rewrite on redirects is disabled, the \u201cskip CA name\u201d check is false, and the service name is **ssl_service_1**. All other boolean flags, such as send\u2011close\u2011notify and SNI enablement, reflect the values listed above."
